.TH CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER \- declare buffer for ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(
    name,
    smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array of the right size for a ranged memory pool
with the given parameters, which the caller then passes to
crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp(). The computed size
includes the status byte of every element of every sub-pool, and the
buffer holds all the sub-pools one after another in contiguous memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                                     Variable name for the buffer
smallest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, depending on where
  you declare it
It is useful on embedded systems, and anywhere you don't want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry of
  the pool is guaranteed to have, which is 16 bytes, so you can pass
  the buffer straight to crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp()
  without thinking about alignment, and every sub-pool segment inside
  the buffer stays correctly aligned too. The 16 is a fixed constant;
  CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(3) explains why
The buffer has exactly the size that
  crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp() requires for the
  same parameters
A _Static_assert rejects three cases at compile time: Parameters that
  would make the required buffer size overflow size_t, a
  number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two below
  largest_size_power_of_two - smallest_size_power_of_two, and a
  smallest_size_power_of_two below 4. The smallest element that a
  ranged pool serves is 16 bytes, so a buffer declared for anything
  smaller could only go to a constructor that refuses it. Without the
  assert, the macro would silently give an array of the wrong size
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(my_buffer, 4, 8, 10);
crmempool *rmp = crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 4, 8, 10,
    crmempool_fallback_disabled, false, NULL);
if (!rmp)
    return 1;
/* ... crmempool_alloc_entry(rmp, n) / crmempool_free_entry(rmp, e) ... */
crmempool_destroy(rmp);   /* frees the pool object, not my_buffer */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp (3)
